1 SYNCHRONIZE

   Waits for a batch job to end.  The job's final status becomes
   $STATUS (with its message shown, if it isn't a success), so a
   procedure can submit a job and act on how it ended.  The other
   processes run while it waits; Ctrl/Y or Ctrl/C ends the wait.

   Format

     SYNCHRONIZE [job-name] [/ENTRY=n] [/QUEUE=queue-name]

2 Parameter

 job-name

   The name of one of your own jobs, in /QUEUE's queue (SYS$BATCH by
   default).  Give it or /ENTRY.

2 Description

   A job that isn't there is %JBC-E-NOSUCHENT by entry number and
   %JBC-E-NOSUCHJOB by name.  Another user's job needs read access to it
   (%JBC-E-NOPRIV).  A job already retained gives its status at once.  A
   job that has ended and gone gives its status to the first SYNCHRONIZE
   that asks for it, and is NOSUCHENT afterwards.

   $STATUS is the job's status with its message bit (%X10000000) set:
   %X10000001 for a job that succeeded, %X10048084 for one STOP/QUEUE/ENTRY
   or DELETE/ENTRY ended (JOBABORT), %X100480EC for one STOP/QUEUE/RESET
   ended (QUERESET).

2 Qualifiers
/ENTRY

   /ENTRY=n

   The job's entry number, as SUBMIT gave it in $ENTRY.

/QUEUE

   /QUEUE=queue-name

   The queue to find job-name in; SYS$BATCH by default.

2 Examples

   1.$ SUBMIT/NOPRINTER BUILD
     Job BUILD (queue SYS$BATCH, entry 12) started on SYS$BATCH
     $ SYNCHRONIZE/ENTRY='$ENTRY'
     $ SHOW SYMBOL $STATUS
       $STATUS == "%X10000001"

     Runs BUILD.COM as a job and waits for it to end.
